## The idea

We want the longest run of cells that contains at most `material` ocean cells (each ocean cell can be filled in). That is a **sliding window**: extend the right edge one cell at a time; whenever the window holds too many ocean cells, move the left edge forward until it is affordable again. The longest window seen is the answer. Every follow-up keeps this shape: walk the array twice for the circular version, restart past obstacles, and count cost instead of cells for the weighted version.

## Step by step

1. left = 0, ocean = 0, best = 0.
2. For each right: if cells[right] is ocean, ocean++.
3. While ocean > material: if cells[left] is ocean, ocean--; left++.
4. The window [left, right] is now affordable: best = max(best, right − left + 1).
5. Circular: if all ocean fits, the answer is n. Otherwise run right from 0 to 2n−1 using index right % n, and also shrink when the window grows longer than n.
6. Obstacles (−1): when right hits an obstacle, start a fresh window just after it.
7. Weighted: add cost[right] instead of 1, and shrink while the total cost > budget.

## Worked example

**Input:** cells = [1,0,0,1,1,0,1], material = 1

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| right 0..1 | window [1,0] → 1 ocean → best 2 |
| right 2 | [1,0,0] → 2 ocean > 1 → shrink: drop 1, drop 0 → window [0] (index 2), ocean 1 |
| right 3..4 | window [0,1,1] → best 3 |
| right 5 | adds ocean → 2 > 1 → shrink past index 2 → window [1,1,0] (3..5) |
| right 6 | window [1,1,0,1] (3..6) → length 4 → best 4 |

**Result:** 4

## Cost

O(n) time — each pointer moves at most n (2n in the circular version) steps. O(1) extra memory.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
public static class ArtificialIsland {

    // 1 = land, 0 = ocean. Convert at most `material` ocean cells. Longest run of land?
    public static int MaxIsland(int[] cells, int material) {
        if (cells == null || cells.Length == 0) return 0;
        int best = 0, ocean = 0, left = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < cells.Length; right++) {
            if (cells[right] == 0) ocean++;
            while (ocean > material) {                 // too much ocean: shrink from the left
                if (cells[left] == 0) ocean--;
                left++;
            }
            int length = right - left + 1;
            if (length > best) best = length;
        }
        return best;
    }

    // The array is a ring: the island may wrap from the end to the start.
    public static int MaxIslandCircular(int[] cells, int material) {
        if (cells == null || cells.Length == 0) return 0;
        int n = cells.Length;

        int totalOcean = 0;
        foreach (int cell in cells) if (cell == 0) totalOcean++;
        if (totalOcean <= material) return n;          // the whole ring fits

        int best = 0, ocean = 0, left = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < 2 * n; right++) {  // walk the ring twice
            if (cells[right % n] == 0) ocean++;
            while (ocean > material || right - left + 1 > n) {   // never longer than the ring
                if (cells[left % n] == 0) ocean--;
                left++;
            }
            int length = right - left + 1;
            if (length > best) best = length;
        }
        return best;
    }

    // -1 is an obstacle: never land, never convertible. A window can never contain one.
    public static int MaxIslandWithObstacles(int[] cells, int material) {
        if (cells == null || cells.Length == 0) return 0;
        int best = 0, ocean = 0, left = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < cells.Length; right++) {
            if (cells[right] == -1) {                  // wall: start again just after it
                left = right + 1;
                ocean = 0;
                continue;
            }
            if (cells[right] == 0) ocean++;
            while (ocean > material) {
                if (cells[left] == 0) ocean--;
                left++;
            }
            int length = right - left + 1;
            if (length > best) best = length;
        }
        return best;
    }

    // Each ocean cell i costs cost[i] to convert; spend at most `budget`.
    public static int MaxIslandWeighted(int[] cells, int[] cost, int budget) {
        if (cells == null || cells.Length == 0) return 0;
        int best = 0, left = 0;
        long spent = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < cells.Length; right++) {
            if (cells[right] == 0) spent += cost[right];
            while (spent > budget) {
                if (cells[left] == 0) spent -= cost[left];
                left++;
            }
            int length = right - left + 1;
            if (length > best) best = length;
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Empty array? All ocean? Material larger than the array?**

0; min(material, n); n

**Q: Brute force vs this?**

O(n²) or O(n³) vs O(n) window

**Q: Why does a window work?**

The constraint is monotone: a window valid now stays valid when shrunk

**Q: Is there shared state?**

No — pure function; say so

**Q: Ten billion cells streamed from disk.**

The window is O(1) state — stream it

**Q: How would you test it?**

Brute-force oracle on random small arrays
